### Check for existing issues - [x] I have searched the existing issues and checked that my issue is not a duplicate. Related but different: #36066 covers the Realtime API never resolving a named credential at all. This one resolves the credential, then skips any value the deployment happens to store as a blank string. ### What happened? A model that takes its key from a stored credential can also carry its own `api_base`. When that stored `api_base` is an empty string, the credential's `api_base` is never applied and the request goes to the provider's default endpoint instead. The dashboard is what puts the empty string there. The model edit form submits every connection field on save, so a model whose API Base box is blank (because the endpoint lives on the credential) gets saved with `api_base: ""`. The add-model form drops blank fields, so a freshly created copy of the same deployment works fine. That asymmetry makes it look like the credential is healthy and only one particular model is cursed. What I expected: a credential fills in whatever the deployment did not specify, and a blank string is not a specification, so the request should land on the endpoint stored on the credential. The visible symptom depends on where the proxy runs. Where the provider default is reachable you get a fast 401 quoting a provider you never configured. Where it is not reachable, the request hangs until the client gives up, so it reads as a timeout, and rotating the credential's key looks like it changed nothing. ### User Flow Before a (hypothetical) fix: an admin rotates a stored credential, one model stays broken, and a brand new copy of that same model works 1. They open https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models, open a model that takes its key from a stored credential, and save it with the API Base box left empty 2. They open that credential on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=credentials and set the new key and endpoint on it 3. They send POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions naming that model 4. The call never reaches the endpoint stored on the credential. It comes back 401 quoting a provider they never configured, or it hangs until their client gives up 5. They add the same deployment again on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models as a new model against the same credential, and that copy answers 200 After a (hypothetical) fix: the rotation takes effect on the model they already had 1. They open https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models, open a model that takes its key from a stored credential, and save it with the API Base box left empty 2. They open that credential on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=credentials and set the new key and endpoint on it 3. They send POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions naming that model 4. The call answers 200 from the endpoint stored on the credential, with real token usage 5. They add the same deployment again on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models as a new model against the same credential, and that copy answers 200 ### Proof the bug occurs Config / setup the proxy ran with, `/tmp/blank_api_base_repro.yaml`. `edited-model` carries the blank API base a dashboard save leaves behind, `newly-added-model` is the same deployment without it: ```yaml credential_list: - credential_name: openai-compat-cred credential_info: provider: openai credential_values: api_base: https://api.deepseek.com/v1 api_key: os.environ/DEEPSEEK_API_KEY model_list: - model_name: edited-model litellm_params: model: openai/deepseek-chat api_base: "" litellm_credential_name: openai-compat-cred - model_name: newly-added-model litellm_params: model: openai/deepseek-chat litellm_credential_name: openai-compat-cred general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 ``` Env vars: `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` set to a real DeepSeek key (redacted below where the upstream echoed it back). No other env vars set. Version or commit: v1.98.0, captured at bc6e7df05b. Proxy started with: ```bash uv run --frozen python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config /tmp/blank_api_base_repro.yaml --port 4000 ``` Case 1, the model saved with a blank API base: ```bash curl -s http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"edited-model","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with the single word: ok"}],"max_tokens":8}' \ -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" ``` Output. The DeepSeek key went to OpenAI, so the request never reached the endpoint on the credential: ``` {"error":{"message":"litellm.AuthenticationError: AuthenticationError: OpenAIException - Incorrect API key provided: sk-REDACTED. You can find your API key at https://platform.openai.com/account/api-keys.. Received Model Group=edited-model
Available Model Group Fallbacks=None","type":null,"param":null,"code":"401"}} HTTP 401 ``` Case 2, the same deployment created without an API base: ```bash curl -s http://localhost:4000/v1/chat/completions \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"newly-added-model","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with the single word: ok"}],"max_tokens":8}' \ -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" ``` Output, answered by the credential's endpoint: ``` {"id":"4aac7f1d-0051-4e24-87b8-484cb335fac9","created":1786954807,"model":"newly-added-model","object":"chat.completion","system_fingerprint":"a26a7955944dc5c60445bff77fac9c8e","choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"ok","role":"assistant","provider_specific_fields":{"refusal":null}},"provider_specific_fields":{}}],"usage":{"completion_tokens":1,"prompt_tokens":11,"total_tokens":12,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":0,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":11}} HTTP 200 ``` The only difference between the two deployments is the blank `api_base`, and it is the one that fails. ### What part of LiteLLM is this about? Proxy ### What LiteLLM version are you on ? v1.98.0 (bc6e7df05b)
